@startuml
title DMX512-A at 250 kbaud — the frame layer, and the one counted cell every byte uses
' Sources: firmware/dmx512.pe (break/mark loops, tx_byte's 11 cells, the page
'          arithmetic), tb/tb_pe_soc_dmx512.v (the lean receiver, the two
'          floors, the per-slot comparison), tools/fw/peasm.py (SDA.. no:
'          TX is bit 0, the shared 8-bit port),
'          reviews/2026-09-25/FW-BUS-BLOCK3-FINDINGS.md (the measured numbers)
' Program: break 22 cells, mark 3 cells, START code 0x00, then 512 data slots
skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam state {
  FontSize 11
  BackgroundColor<<brk>>  #f6d9d9
  BorderColor<<brk>>      #b05050
  FontColor<<brk>>        #6a2020
  BackgroundColor<<slot>> #dce9f8
  BorderColor<<slot>>     #4a6fa5
  FontColor<<slot>>       #16324f
  BackgroundColor<<fold>> #dceee0
  BorderColor<<fold>>     #4a8a5c
  FontColor<<fold>>       #17452a
  BackgroundColor<<box>>  #f2f2f2
  BorderColor<<box>>      #808080
  FontColor<<box>>        #303030
}

state "FRAME LAYER — break, mark, start code, 512 slots" as fr <<box>> {
  state "THE BREAK\nLDI A,0 / OUT TXPIN  — the line going LOW IS the break\nLDI A,22 / STM 6,A\n117 iterations of a 2-instruction body plus\n236 clocks of delay = 240 clocks per iteration\n= 22 x 4.000 us = 88.000 us" as f1 <<brk>>
  state "THE MARK\nLDI A,1 / OUT TXPIN  — released ONCE, here, not in\nthe loop: an OUT inside it would make the mark\ndepend on which iteration it was\nLDI A,3 = 3 x 4.000 = 12.000 us" as f2 <<brk>>
  state "THE START CODE\ndmem[7] = 0, dmem[8] = 0x00\nit goes out through the SAME transmitter as\nthe data slots, so there is one bit format in\nthis file and not two" as f3 <<slot>>
  state "first_data\ndmem[1] = 0, dmem[2] = 0, dmem[9] = 0\nTHE PAGE COUNTER, and this line is the whole\ndifference between a frame that ends and a\ntransmitter that runs for ever. It was left\nuninitialised, so at the first page boundary\nit was x, x+1 is x, and SUB A,2 on an x is an\nx: the frame never stopped" as f4 <<box>>
  state "slots\ndmem[8] = dmem[2]  (the slot value IS the ramp)\ndmem[7] = 1  (this one IS a data slot)" as f5 <<slot>>
  state "slot_done\ndmem[2] + 1  (8 bits, so this wraps at 255 by itself)\ndmem[1] + 1 / JZ page_end\nTHE PAGE COUNTER IS NOT INCREMENTED HERE,\nand the first version did it on every slot,\nwhich ended the frame after TWO data slots —\nbecause 2 was the page count and the page\ncounter was being asked to count slots" as f6 <<box>>
  state "page_end\ndmem[9] + 1 / SUB A,2 / JZ done\n512 data slots = 2 pages of 256" as f7 <<box>>
  state "done\ndmem[10] = 0xA5" as f8
  state "park" as f9
  f1 --> f2
  f2 --> f3
  f3 --> f4
  f4 --> f5
  f5 --> f6
  f6 --> f5 : not a page boundary
  f6 --> f7 : 256 slots
  f7 --> f5 : dmem[9] < 2
  f7 --> f8 : dmem[9] == 2
  f8 --> f9
}

state "TRANSMITTER — 250 kbaud, 8N2, one loop over ELEVEN bit cells" as tx <<box>> {
  state "sbit — the dispatch, and its four different lengths\nLDM A,5 / SUB A,9 / JZ sb_stop\nSUB A,1 / JZ sb_stop2 / JZ sb_start\ncells 1-8: 7 instructions of dispatch then LDM+AND+OUT\ncell 0: the same 7, the last JZ taken\ncell 9: 2 instructions of dispatch, then LDI+OUT\ncell 10: 3 of dispatch, then LDI+OUT" as g0 <<box>>
  state "the DATA branch\nLDM A,0 / AND A,1 / OUT TXPIN — LSB first\nthen LDM/SHR/STM: the shift lives HERE and\nnot in the common tail. On a ramp the LOW\nbits are the difference between a fixture\nthat works and one that does not" as g1 <<slot>>
  state "sb_start — cell 0\nLDI A,0 / OUT TXPIN\n3 NOPs. This dispatch is one instruction\nshorter than a data cell's, and the cell that\nhas to be 240 clocks is the DATA cell after\nit, not this one" as g2 <<brk>>
  state "sb_stop — cell 9\n5 NOPs: the shortest dispatch in the file, and\nthe cell it must make correct is cell 8.\nThen LDI A,1 / OUT, then 2 NOPs, for\ncell 9 rather than this one" as g3 <<brk>>
  state "sb_stop2 — cell 10\n4 NOPs, and they are here for cell 9's sake,\nnot this cell's. FALLS THROUGH into sb_hold:\nthis is the last cell of a slot, so it has\nno successor whose length has to match" as g4 <<brk>>
  state "sb_hold\nLDI A,109  (1 clock) then SUB A,1 / JNZ\n= 109 x 2 = 218.  21 + 1 + 218 = 240 clocks\n= 4.000 us = exactly 250,000 baud\n218 = 2 x 109 and 109 is prime: this or a\n109-instruction body run twice, which is the\nsame arithmetic written in 107 NOPs" as g5 <<box>>
  g0 --> g1
  g0 --> g2
  g0 --> g3
  g0 --> g4
  g1 --> g5
  g2 --> g5
  g3 --> g5
  g4 --> g5
  g5 --> g0 : dmem[4]--, dmem[5]++
}

state "slot_out — ONE EXIT FOR ALL 513 SLOTS\nNO RETURN POINT AND NO DISPATCH: the frame layer\nJMPs in and this block falls out of the\ntransmitter's last cell, so the continuation IS\nthe program counter. Noticing that the control\nflow already provides the thing the ISA lacks is\nworth more than the dmem slot would have been.\ndmem[3]++ then dmem[7] decides what leaving means" as so <<box>>

[*] --> fr
fr --> tx
tx --> so
so --> f5 : dmem[7] = 1
so --> f4 : dmem[7] = 0, the start code's exit

note right of so
  THE START CODE'S EXIT MUST NOT ADVANCE THE
  RAMP. The payload is deliberately "data slot k
  has value k", so the first data slot is 0x00,
  and an exit that advanced the ramp on the way
  past the start code would make the first data
  slot 0x01 and the payload "k + 1" - still a
  ramp, still a legal fixture, and one index out
  of step with every document describing it. The
  first version had exactly that bug and the TB
  caught it as a single mismatched slot, which is
  the cheapest possible way to catch an
  off-by-one in a 513-slot frame.
end note
@enduml

@startuml
title DMX512-A — one frame is a break, a mark, a START code and 512 slots
skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam ParticipantBackgroundColor #eef3fb
skinparam ParticipantBorderColor #4a6fa5
skinparam ParticipantFontColor #16324f
skinparam ActorBackgroundColor #fbe9cf
skinparam ActorBorderColor #b8813a
skinparam ActorFontColor #5a3d12
skinparam SequenceGroupBackgroundColor #fbfbfb
skinparam SequenceGroupBorderColor #b0b0b0
skinparam ArrowColor #333333

actor "master\npe_soc" as M
participant "wire\nbit 0" as W
actor "receiver" as R

M -> W : BREAK — 22 cells of LOW = 88.06 us measured (floor 87.5)
W -> R : a receiver will not look at a frame that does not begin with a break
M -> W : MARK — 3 cells of HIGH = 12.38 us measured (floor 8.0)
group slot 0: the START code, through the SAME transmitter
  M -> W : 0x00 = 11 cells of 8N2
  note over W
    The start code is what tells a receiver what
    the 512 slots MEAN. 513 slots with no start
    code is the most common way to end up with a
    working fixture that lights nothing.
  end note
end
group slots 1..512: a wrapping 8-bit ramp, two pages of 256
  M -> W : slot 1 = 0x01 ... slot 128 = 0x80 ... slot 256 = 0x00 ... slot 512 = 0xFF
  note over M
    An 8-bit register incremented with an 8-bit
    add IS a wrapping ramp, so the payload costs
    two instructions per slot to produce, and the
    TB recomputes all 512 independently rather
    than trusting a 513-entry list of its own.
  end note
  note over M, R
    AND THE BIT ORDER IS NOT A PALINDROME ANYWHERE:
    slot 1 is 0x01 and slot 128 is 0x80, so a
    transmitter that shifted the wrong way produces
    0x80 and 0x01 - swapped, not identical. That is
    what makes the per-slot comparison worth
    anything, and it is why the payload is a ramp
    and not a constant.
  end note
end

note over M, R
  513 x 11 x 4.000 us = 22.572 ms of frame, plus the break and the mark.
  The TB's drain window is derived from that: 26 ms, which is 15% of headroom
  over a number that is computed rather than guessed. The first version of a
  testbench that guessed allowed 900 us — a twentieth of the frame — and
  reported a truncated stream as if it were a protocol failure.

  HOW A SLOT'S START BIT IS FOUND, since a falling edge is not one: within a
  slot of 8N2 the LAST possible falling edge is at cell 8, because cells 9 and
  10 are both stop bits and cannot fall. A fall at least 9.5 cells after the
  current slot's start bit is therefore not inside that slot, and the next
  slot's start bit is the only thing it can be. The first slot is anchored on
  the BREAK, which is 88 us of LOW and is not confusable with anything.
end note
@enduml

@startuml
title DMX512-A — the three START-code ranges, and the one this firmware sends
skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam ParticipantBackgroundColor #eef3fb
skinparam ParticipantBorderColor #4a6fa5
skinparam ParticipantFontColor #16324f
skinparam ActorBackgroundColor #fbe9cf
skinparam ActorBorderColor #b8813a
skinparam ActorFontColor #5a3d12
skinparam SequenceGroupBackgroundColor #fbfbfb
skinparam SequenceGroupBorderColor #b0b0b0
skinparam ArrowColor #333333

participant "START code\nslot 0" as S0
participant "0x00\nall-slot" as R00 #dceee0
participant "0x01..0x7F\nfirst 1..127 slots" as R01
participant "0x80..0xFF\nstart address" as R80
actor "receiver" as RX

S0 -> RX : slot 0 arrives
group the all-slot code — the ONLY one this firmware sends
  S0 -> R00 : 0x00
  R00 -> RX : every one of the 512 data slots is a\ndimmer or fixture level; no follow-up slot
end
group the patch-cable range
  S0 -> R01 : 0x01 .. 0x7F
  R01 -> RX : the following slots are patch-cable\npositions: code 0x2A starts at slot 42
end
group the start-address range
  S0 -> R80 : 0x80 .. 0xFF
  R80 -> RX : the NEXT TWO slots are one big-endian\n16-bit slot number, and the frame starts there
end

note over S0, RX
  DMX512-A defines exactly these three ranges, and the START code is what tells a
  receiver what the 512 slots MEAN. Sending 512 slots with no START code is the
  most common way to end up with a working fixture that lights nothing.

  THIS FIRMWARE SENDS 0x00 AND NOTHING ELSE, and it goes out through the SAME
  transmitter as the data slots — so there is one bit format in the file rather
  than two, and a START code sent by a different code path is a START code that
  can disagree with the slots.

  The TB's expected-slot function returns 0x00 for slot 0 and (n-1) & 0xFF for
  every slot after it, so a START code from either of the other two ranges would
  be caught on slot 0 and on the slot count that follows.
end note
note over R00
  WHY 0x00 IS THE RIGHT CHOICE FOR A
  FIXTURE: it is the only range that
  needs no follow-up slot, so all 512
  payload slots are payload. A
  start-address code would spend two of
  the 513 on the address.
end note
@enduml

